@num 3
@title The Vapnik–Chervonenkis dimension
@titlehtml The <em>Vapnik–Chervonenkis</em> dimension
@pages 27–38
@dek Occam’s razor counts hypotheses, which is useless when there are infinitely many. The VC dimension measures a class by the largest set of points it can label in every possible way, and that number turns out to control exactly how much data learning needs.
---
<role r="question" a="what replaces log |H| for infinite classes?">
Theorem 2.2 needs $\log|\H|$ to be small, so it says nothing about infinite classes. Yet the classes used most in practice are infinite, even uncountable: linear threshold functions (halfspaces) in $\R^n$, and the rectangles of Chapter 1, which we learned with no trouble. This chapter replaces $\log|\H|$ by a measure of capacity that can be finite for infinite classes, and shows it controls the sample complexity from above and below.
</role>

## 3.1 The Vapnik Chervonenkis (VC) Dimension {27} [d2 i3]
<role r="setup" a="dichotomies">

We drop the subscript $n$ throughout; everything applies to $\C=\bigcup_n\C_n$. Let $S\subseteq\X$ be a finite set of instances. A concept $c$ restricted to $S$ is the function $c|_S:S\to\{0,1\}$, and the set of all such restrictions is

$$#eq3-1 \Pi_\C(S)=\{c|_S:c\in\C\}. \tag{3.1}$$

If $S=\{x_1,\dots,x_m\}$ we can list each restriction as the vector of its values:

$$#eq3-2 \Pi_\C(S)=\{(c(x_1),\dots,c(x_m)):c\in\C\}. \tag{3.2}$$

These are the **dichotomies** of $S$ that $\C$ can produce: the ways of splitting $S$ into a positive part and a negative part. There are $2^m$ splits in all, so $|\Pi_\C(S)|\le2^m$, however large $\C$ is. That is the key: an infinite class can only act on $m$ points in finitely many ways.
</role>

<thm k="Definition 3.1" nm="Shattering" pg="28">
A finite set $S\subseteq\X$ is **shattered** by $\C$ if $|\Pi_\C(S)|=2^{|S|}$, that is, if every dichotomy of $S$ is realised by some concept in $\C$.
</thm>

<thm k="Definition 3.2" nm="Vapnik–Chervonenkis dimension" pg="28">
The **VC dimension** of $\C$, written $\VCD(\C)$, is the size $d$ of the largest finite set shattered by $\C$. If $\C$ shatters arbitrarily large finite sets, $\VCD(\C)=\infty$.
</thm>
<role r="caution" a="mind the quantifiers">

The quantifiers matter. To show $\VCD(\C)\ge d$ you need *one* set of $d$ points that is shattered. To show $\VCD(\C)<d+1$ you need that *every* set of $d+1$ points has some labelling that no concept produces. In the language of the game: an adversary picks the labels, you pick the points first.
</role>

### 3.1.1 Examples
<role r="example" a="intervals: VC dimension 2">

**Intervals on the line.** Let $\X=\R$ and let $\C$ consist of the indicators of closed intervals $[a,b]$. Any two points can be shattered: an interval can contain both, either one, or neither. No three points $x_1<x_2<x_3$ can be: the labelling $+,-,+$ would need an interval containing $x_1$ and $x_3$ but not $x_2$, which lies between them. So $\VCD=2$.
</role>
<role r="example" a="rectangles: VC dimension 4">

**Axis-aligned rectangles in the plane.** Four points in a diamond (one at each compass point) are shattered: for any subset, the smallest rectangle around it misses the others. Four points in some other positions are not, so the choice of set matters, but one good set is enough. No five points can be shattered. Among five points, take one with the largest $x$-coordinate, one with the smallest, one with the largest $y$ and one with the smallest. That is at most four points, so some fifth point is none of these. Label it negative and the others positive: any rectangle containing the four extreme points contains the whole bounding box, and with it the fifth point. So $\VCD=4$.
</role>
<role r="example" a="halfplanes: VC dimension 3">

**Halfplanes.** A **linear threshold function** on $\R^2$ is positive on one side of a line and negative on the other. Three points not on a line are shattered. Three points on a line are not ($+,-,+$ fails again), but again one good set is enough. No four points are shattered. If their convex hull is a quadrilateral, label opposite corners alike and adjacent corners differently: the two positive points' segment crosses the two negative points' segment, so no line separates them. If the hull is a triangle with the fourth point inside, label the corners positive and the inside point negative. (Points with three on a line are handled like the collinear triple.) So $\VCD=3$. In $\R^n$ the answer is $n+1$ (Exercise 3.1 in the notes; Sheet 3).
</role>

Figure 3.1 lets you test sets of points against all three classes. The grid lists every dichotomy and marks the ones the class can produce.

@@clt-fig3-1@@

<pause n="Thresholds and their VC dimension" q="On $\X=\R$, let $\C$ be the thresholds $c_t(x)=\ind[x\ge t]$, $t\in\R$. What is $\VCD(\C)$? And for the class of 'one-sided intervals' $\{\ind[x\ge t]\}\cup\{\ind[x\le t]\}$?">
A single point can be labelled either way, so $\VCD(\C)\ge1$. For two points $x_1<x_2$, the labelling $x_1\mapsto1$, $x_2\mapsto0$ is impossible, since $x_1\ge t$ forces $x_2\ge t$. So $\VCD(\C)=1$, although $\C$ is uncountable. With thresholds in both directions, two points are shattered ($c_t$ handles three labellings and $\ind[x\le t]$ handles $1,0$). Three points $x_1<x_2<x_3$ are not: $+,-,+$ is impossible for either kind. So the VC dimension is $2$.
</pause>

<sheet n="2" qs="2">Part 1 only needs the definition of shattering.</sheet>

