## 3.2 Growth Function {30} [d3 i3]
<role r="question" a="how many dichotomies on m points?">

How many dichotomies can $\C$ produce on $m$ points, at most? For $m\le\VCD(\C)$ the answer is $2^m$. The surprise is what happens after that: the count stops growing exponentially and grows only polynomially, with degree $\VCD(\C)$.
</role>

<thm k="Definition 3.3" nm="Growth function" pg="30">
For $m\in\N$, the **growth function** of $\C$ is $$\Pi_\C(m)=\max\{|\Pi_\C(S)|:S\subseteq\X,\ |S|=m\}.$$
</thm>

<thm k="Lemma 3.4" nm="Sauer–Shelah lemma" pg="30" it>
If $\VCD(\C)=d$, then for all $m\ge d$, $$\Pi_\C(m)\le\Big(\frac{em}d\Big)^d.$$
</thm>
<role r="reading" a="Lemma 3.4">

So a class of VC dimension $d$ behaves, on $m$ points, like a finite class of size about $m^d$. The proof goes through a counting function.
</role>

<thm k="Definition 3.5" d="Φ(m, d): subsets of size at most d" pg="30">
For $m,d\in\N$ let $$#eq3-3 \Phi(m,d)=\sum_{i=0}^d\binom mi. \tag{3.3}$$
</thm>
<role r="idea" a="what Φ counts">

$\Phi(m,d)$ counts the subsets of an $m$-element set that have at most $d$ elements. Clearly $\Phi(m,0)=\Phi(0,d)=1$, $\Phi$ increases in both arguments, and $\Phi(m,d)=2^m$ when $m\le d$.
</role>

<thm k="Lemma 3.6" d="Pascal’s rule and a bound for Φ" pg="30" it>
For all $m,d\ge1$, $$#eq3-4 \Phi(m,d)=\Phi(m-1,d)+\Phi(m-1,d-1), \tag{3.4}$$ and for $m\ge d\ge1$, $$#eq3-5 \Phi(m,d)\le\Big(\frac{em}d\Big)^d. \tag{3.5}$$
</thm>

<pf>
For (3.4), count subsets of $\{1,\dots,m\}$ of size at most $d$ by whether they contain $m$. Those that don't are subsets of $\{1,\dots,m-1\}$ of size at most $d$: there are $\Phi(m-1,d)$. Those that do are $\{m\}$ together with a subset of $\{1,\dots,m-1\}$ of size at most $d-1$: there are $\Phi(m-1,d-1)$. (The notes do the same with Pascal's rule $\binom mi=\binom{m-1}i+\binom{m-1}{i-1}$.)

For (3.5), multiply and divide by $(m/d)^d$. Since $d/m\le1$ and $i\le d$, we have $(d/m)^d\le(d/m)^i$, so $$\Phi(m,d)=\Big(\frac md\Big)^d\sum_{i=0}^d\binom mi\Big(\frac dm\Big)^d\le\Big(\frac md\Big)^d\sum_{i=0}^m\binom mi\Big(\frac dm\Big)^i=\Big(\frac md\Big)^d\Big(1+\frac dm\Big)^m\le\Big(\frac md\Big)^de^d,$$ using the binomial theorem and $1+x\le e^x$.
</pf>

<thm k="Lemma 3.7" d="Growth function is at most Φ(m, d)" pg="31" it>
If $\VCD(\C)=d$ then $\Pi_\C(m)\le\Phi(m,d)$ for all $m$.
</thm>
<role r="roadmap" a="how the pieces fit">

Lemmas 3.6 and 3.7 together prove the Sauer–Shelah lemma.
</role>

<pf>
Induction on $m+d$. If $d=0$, no single point is shattered, so all concepts agree on every point and $\C$ produces one dichotomy on any set: $\Pi_\C(m)=1=\Phi(m,0)$. If $m=0$ there is one dichotomy of the empty set and $\Phi(0,d)=1$.

Now take $S$ with $|S|=m$ and a point $x\in S$. Every dichotomy of $S$ restricts to a dichotomy of $S\setminus\{x\}$. Some dichotomies of $S\setminus\{x\}$ extend in only one way (the class forces the label of $x$), others in two ways ($x$ can go either way). So $$#eq3-7 |\Pi_\C(S)|=|\Pi_\C(S\setminus\{x\})|+|\{\text{dichotomies of }S\setminus\{x\}\text{ that extend both ways}\}|. \tag{3.7}$$ The first term is at most $\Pi_\C(m-1)\le\Phi(m-1,d)$ by induction.

For the second term, let $\C'$ be the class (on $S\setminus\{x\}$) of dichotomies that extend both ways. If $\C'$ shatters a set $S'\subseteq S\setminus\{x\}$, then $\C$ shatters $S'\cup\{x\}$: any labelling of $S'$ comes from $\C'$, and both labels of $x$ are then available. So $|S'|+1\le d$, which means $\VCD(\C')\le d-1$, and by induction the second term is at most $\Phi(m-1,d-1)$. Adding and using (3.4) gives $|\Pi_\C(S)|\le\Phi(m,d)$.
</pf>

<sheet n="2" qs="3"></sheet>

