## 3.3 Sample Complexity Upper Bound {32} [d3 i3]
<role r="link" a="Theorem 2.2, for infinite classes">

The VC dimension now plays the part that $\log|\H|$ played in Theorem 2.2.
</role>

<thm k="Theorem 3.8" d="VC dimension bounds the sample size above" pg="32" it>
Let $\H\supseteq\C$ be a hypothesis class with $\VCD(\H)=d$, where $1\le d<\infty$, and let $L$ be a consistent learner for $\C$ that outputs hypotheses in $\H$. There is a universal constant $\kappa_0$ such that, for all $0<\eps,\delta\le\frac12$, $L$ PAC learns $\C$ when given $$m\ge\kappa_0\Big(\frac1\eps\log\frac1\delta+\frac d\eps\log\frac1\eps\Big)$$ examples from $\EX(c,\D)$.
</thm>
<role r="reading" a="Theorem 3.8">

Compare with (2.3): $\log|\H|$ has become $d\log\frac1\eps$. The proof has a new idea in it, **symmetrisation**, which turns up in almost every sample-complexity argument from here on. First some vocabulary.
</role>
<role r="setup" a="disagreements and ε-nets">

For boolean functions $f,g$ write $f\oplus g$ for the function that is $1$ where they disagree. Then $\err(h;c,\D)=\PP_{x\sim\D}[(h\oplus c)(x)=1]$. Let $\H\oplus c=\{h\oplus c:h\in\H\}$; it has the same VC dimension as $\H$, since $h\mapsto h\oplus c$ just flips the labels at the points where $c=1$ (Exercise 3.4 in the notes).

A finite set $S$ is an **$\eps$-net** for $\H\oplus c$ (with respect to $\D$) if every $h\oplus c$ with $\PP_{x\sim\D}[(h\oplus c)(x)=1]\ge\eps$ is $1$ at some point of $S$. In plain terms: every hypothesis with error at least $\eps$ makes a mistake somewhere on $S$. So if the sample is an $\eps$-net, a consistent learner can't output a bad hypothesis, because a bad one is inconsistent. The whole proof is about showing a random sample is an $\eps$-net with probability at least $1-\delta$.
</role>
<role r="idea" a="symmetrisation: a ghost sample">

The difficulty is that there are infinitely many bad hypotheses, so we can't union-bound over them. Symmetrisation gets round this by bringing in a second, imaginary sample, after which only the finitely many dichotomies on $2m$ points matter.
</role>

<bg id="bg-chernoff" t="Chernoff bounds" lead="Background">
Let $X=X_1+\dots+X_m$ be a sum of independent $\{0,1\}$-valued random variables with mean $\mu=\E[X]$. For $0<\eta\le1$, $$\PP[X\le(1-\eta)\mu]\le e^{-\eta^2\mu/2},\qquad\PP[X\ge(1+\eta)\mu]\le e^{-\eta^2\mu/3}.$$ A sum of many independent bits is very unlikely to be far from its mean in relative terms, and the failure probability shrinks exponentially in $\mu$. This is much stronger than what Chebyshev's inequality gives. The notes' Appendix A states several versions.
</bg>

<pf>
**Two samples.** Draw a sample $S_1$ of size $m$ and let $A$ be the event that $S_1$ is not an $\eps$-net. We want $\PP[A]\le\delta$. Now draw a second, independent sample $S_2$ of size $m$; it is only a device for the proof.

**Step 1: $\PP[A]\le2\PP[B]$.** Let $B$ be the event that some $h\oplus c$ is $0$ on all of $S_1$ but $1$ on at least $\eps m/2$ points of $S_2$. Suppose $A$ happens, and fix a witness $\tilde h$: $(\tilde h\oplus c)$ is $0$ on $S_1$ but has mass at least $\eps$. Each point of $S_2$ lands where $\tilde h\oplus c=1$ with probability at least $\eps$, so the number $X$ of such points has mean at least $\eps m$, and the Chernoff bound gives $\PP[X<\eps m/2]\le e^{-\eps m/8}\le\frac12$ when $\eps m\ge16$. So given $A$, $B$ happens with probability at least $\frac12$, which means $\PP[B]\ge\frac12\PP[A]$.

**Step 2: bound $\PP[B]$ by counting.** Here is the symmetrisation. Drawing $S_1$ and $S_2$ independently is the same as drawing $2m$ points $S$ first and then splitting them into two halves uniformly at random. Fix $S$. Only the dichotomies of $S$ matter now, and there are at most $\Pi_{\H\oplus c}(2m)\le(2em/d)^d$ of them. Take one that is $1$ on some $r\ge\eps m/2$ points of $S$. For $B$ to happen through it, all $r$ of those points must land in $S_2$. That is like dealing $2m$ cards, $r$ of them red, into two hands of $m$: the chance that the first hand gets no red card is $$\binom mr\Big/\binom{2m}r=\prod_{i=0}^{r-1}\frac{m-i}{2m-i}\le2^{-r}\le2^{-\eps m/2}.$$ A union bound over the dichotomies gives $$\PP[B]\le\Big(\frac{2em}d\Big)^d2^{-\eps m/2},\qquad\text{so}\qquad\PP[A]\le2\Big(\frac{2em}d\Big)^d2^{-\eps m/2}.$$

**Step 3: solve for $m$.** The right side is polynomial in $m$ times an exponentially small factor, so it drops below $\delta$ once $m$ is a large enough multiple of $\frac d\eps\log\frac1\eps+\frac1\eps\log\frac1\delta$. The notes carry out the calculation in full and find that $$m\ge\frac4{\eps\log2}\max\Big\{8d\log\frac4{\eps\log2},\ \log\frac2\delta\Big\}$$ suffices; this also ensures $\eps m\ge16$.
</pf>
<role r="why" a="why allow a larger H">

The theorem applies with $\H=\C$ for proper learning, but its real use is with a larger $\H$, when a bigger class makes consistent learning efficient. Section 3.5 is an example.
</role>

## 3.4 Sample Complexity Lower Bounds {34} [d3 i2]
<role r="question" a="could a cleverer learner use less data?">

The upper bound would be less interesting if some cleverer algorithm could get by with much less data. It can't. The next theorem holds for *every* learning algorithm, however slow, and whatever hypotheses it outputs.
</role>

<thm k="Theorem 3.9" d="VC dimension bounds the sample size below" pg="35" it>
Let $\VCD(\C)\ge d$ with $d\ge25$. Any PAC learning algorithm for $\C$, using any $\H\supseteq\C$, needs at least $$\max\Big\{\frac{d-1}{32\eps},\ \frac1{4\eps}\log\frac1{4\delta}\Big\}$$ examples.
</thm>
<role r="aside" a="the condition d ≥ 25">

(The condition $d\ge25$ only simplifies the arithmetic; a more careful argument works for $d\ge2$.)
</role>
<role r="idea" a="hide most points where the learner rarely looks">

The idea of the first bound: put almost all the probability on one point $x_1$, and spread a small amount, $8\eps$ in total, over $d-1$ other points that $\C$ shatters. With too few examples the learner sees fewer than half of those $d-1$ points. On the unseen ones it can only guess, and since every labelling of them is possible, it guesses wrong on about half. Each unseen point has mass $\frac{8\eps}{d-1}$, so half of more than $\frac{d-1}2$ unseen points already costs more than $2\eps$ in error.
</role>

<pf>
Suppose $L$ PAC learns $\C$ with $m$ examples, where $m<\frac{d-1}{32\eps}$ (we take $m=\frac{d-1}{32\eps}$ for simplicity). Let $T=\{x_1,\dots,x_d\}$ be shattered by $\C$, and let $\D(x_1)=1-8\eps$ and $\D(x_j)=\frac{8\eps}{d-1}$ for $j\ge2$. Only labellings of $T$ matter, and all $2^d$ occur. We may assume $L$ always labels $x_1$ correctly once it has seen it (if not, patch its output at $x_1$).

**Likely events.** The chance that $x_1$ never appears is $(1-8\eps)^m\le e^{-(d-1)/4}\le e^{-6}$. Let $Z$ count the draws that land in $T\setminus\{x_1\}$, so $\E[Z]=8\eps m=\frac{d-1}4$. By the upper Chernoff bound, $\PP[Z\ge\frac{d-1}2]=\PP[Z\ge2\E Z]\le e^{-(d-1)/12}\le e^{-2}$. Let $E$ be the event that $x_1$ is seen and $Z<\frac{d-1}2$; then $\PP[E]>\frac12$.

**Averaging over targets.** Choose the target $c$ uniformly from the $2^d$ labellings of $T$. Given $E$, the sample shows the labels of fewer than $\frac{d+1}2$ points of $T$, and the labels of the others are still uniformly random and independent of everything $L$ has seen. On each unseen point, whatever $h$ says is wrong with probability $\frac12$. More than $\frac{d-1}2$ points of $T\setminus\{x_1\}$ are unseen, so $$\E[\err(h)\mid E]>\frac12\cdot\frac{d-1}2\cdot\frac{8\eps}{d-1}=2\eps.$$ Since this is an average over $c$, there is a fixed target $c$ with $\E[\err(h)\mid E]>2\eps$.

**From average to probability.** Given $E$, $h$ is right at $x_1$, so $\err(h)\le8\eps$ always. If $\err(h)\le\eps$ had conditional probability more than $\frac67$, the conditional mean would be at most $\frac67\eps+\frac17\cdot8\eps=2\eps$, a contradiction. So given $E$, $\err(h)>\eps$ with probability at least $\frac17$, and overall with probability at least $\frac12\cdot\frac17=\frac1{14}$. A PAC learner must succeed for every $\delta$, including any $\delta<\frac1{14}$, so $L$ cannot manage with this many examples.

**The second bound** uses two points. Since $\VCD(\C)\ge2$ there are $x_1,x_2$ and concepts $c_1,c_2$ with $c_1(x_1)=c_2(x_1)=0$ and $c_1(x_2)\neq c_2(x_2)$. Put $\D(x_1)=e^{-4\eps}$ and $\D(x_2)=1-e^{-4\eps}$. A sample of size $\frac1{4\eps}\log\frac1{4\delta}$ misses $x_2$ entirely with probability $(e^{-4\eps})^m=4\delta$. Then the learner can't tell $c_1$ from $c_2$, and for one of them it errs at $x_2$, which has mass $1-e^{-4\eps}>2\eps$ when $\eps<\frac14$. Arguing as above, it fails with probability more than $\delta$.
</pf>
<role r="payoff" a="VC dimension is the statistical story">

So, up to the $\log\frac1\eps$ factor, $\frac d\eps+\frac1\eps\log\frac1\delta$ examples are both necessary and sufficient. The VC dimension captures the *statistical* difficulty of learning a class essentially completely. It says nothing about the *computational* difficulty, which is the subject of Chapter 5.
</role>

<pause n="Lower bound for 3-CNF" q="Chapter 2 claimed that learning 3-CNF needs $\Omega(n^3/\eps)$ examples whatever the algorithm. Using Theorem 3.9, what do you need to show about 3-CNF?">
You need $\VCD(3\text{-CNF}_n)=\Omega(n^3)$: a set of order $n^3$ points shattered by 3-CNF formulas. Here is one. For each clause $C$ made of three distinct variables, $C=z_i\vee z_j\vee z_k$, let $x_C$ be the instance that is $0$ in positions $i,j,k$ and $1$ elsewhere. $x_C$ falsifies $C$ and satisfies every other such clause (each other clause has a variable outside $\{i,j,k\}$, which is $1$). So for any subset $A$ of these instances, the 3-CNF formula made of the clauses $C$ with $x_C\in A$ is $0$ exactly on $A$. That shatters $\binom n3=\Omega(n^3)$ points, and Theorem 3.9 gives the $\Omega(n^3/\eps)$ bound.
</pause>

## 3.5 Consistent Learner for Linear Threshold Functions {36} [d2 i2]
<role r="roadmap" a="halfspaces via linear programming">

To finish, an application. The linear threshold functions on $\R^n$ are

$$#eq3-8 \mathrm{LTF}_n=\{x\mapsto\ind_{\ge0}(w\cdot x+w_0):w\in\R^n,\ \|w\|_2=1,\ w_0\in\R\}, \tag{3.8}$$

where $\ind_{\ge0}(z)=1$ if $z\ge0$ and $0$ otherwise. The VC dimension is $n+1$ (Sheet 3), so by Theorem 3.8 an *efficient consistent learner* gives an efficient PAC learner. Given examples labelled by some halfspace, we need to find a halfspace that agrees with all of them. This is a **linear program**, and linear programs can be solved in polynomial time.
</role>

<bg id="bg-lp" t="Linear programming" lead="Background">
A **linear program** asks for real numbers satisfying finitely many linear inequalities, often while maximising a linear function. The feasible set is a convex polyhedron. Algorithms such as the ellipsoid method and interior-point methods find a feasible point, or report there is none, in time polynomial in the size of the input. That is all we need: here there is nothing to maximise, only a feasible point to find.
</bg>
<role r="setup" a="the linear program">

The variables are $w_0,w_1,\dots,w_n$, and the constraints are

$$#eq3-9 w_0+w_1x_{i1}+\dots+w_nx_{in}\ge0\qquad\text{for each }i\text{ with }y_i=1, \tag{3.9}$$

$$#eq3-10 -w_0-w_1x_{i1}-\dots-w_nx_{in}\ge1\qquad\text{for each }i\text{ with }y_i=0. \tag{3.10}$$
</role>
<role r="technique" a="no strict inequalities: rescale">

A negative example needs $w\cdot x+w_0<0$, but a linear program can't use strict inequalities, since its feasible set has to be closed. Asking for $\le-1$ instead costs nothing, because the inequalities can be rescaled. If the target is $(w^*,w_0^*)$, let $\alpha=\min\{-(w^*\cdot x_i+w_0^*):y_i=0\}$, which is positive. Then $(w^*/\alpha,w_0^*/\alpha)$ satisfies every constraint: positives keep their sign, and negatives now have $-(w\cdot x_i+w_0)\ge1$. So the program is feasible, any feasible point is a consistent halfspace (after rescaling $w$ to unit length), and LTFs are efficiently PAC learnable.
</role>
<role r="example" a="four points in space">

Figure 3.3 shows $n+1=4$ points in $\R^3$ in general position. Every one of the $2^4$ labellings is cut out by a plane, so the four points are shattered.
</role>

<sheet n="3" qs="1">Figure 3.3 is part 1 for $n=3$.</sheet>

@@clt-fig3-3@@
